<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1259</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Badr al-Din Lu'lu' was a freed slave of the Zengid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> who became regent for their young heirs and, from 1234, ruler of Mosul in his own name. He governed for about forty years, patronising metalwork and architecture, and submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. His sons lost Mosul after his death, and it was sacked by the Mongols in 1262.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu.jpg" alt="Badr al-Din Lu'lu'" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 13th century painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badr_al_din_lu%27lu%27_(frontispiece).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
